lessons=[]
specs=[
('two-pointers','Two pointers, one direction','Arrays','Two pointers turn a search over pairs into a sequence of informed decisions. On a sorted array, increasing the left pointer increases the candidate sum; decreasing the right pointer decreases it. That monotonic relationship is what makes discarding candidates safe.','At every step, all discarded pairs have been ruled out by the ordering. Sorting is a prerequisite, and its cost belongs in the analysis.','Moving both pointers without comparing the sum can skip a valid pair. Repeated values still occupy different positions.', 'O(n) after sorting','O(1)',9,['Sort the values','while left < right:','  compare values[left] + values[right]','  if equal: return the pair','  move the pointer that improves the sum'],['Because the input is ordered','Because both pointers are random','Because all pairs have equal sums'],0),
('sliding-window','A little less work, every time','Arrays','Suppose you need the largest sum of three consecutive values. You could add every group from scratch. But adjacent windows share almost everything. Keep a running sum: add the value entering from the right, then remove the value leaving from the left.','The running sum is exactly the sum of the current window. A fixed-size window is a different problem from a variable-length window whose validity depends on a condition.','Updating the best answer before a full window exists gives the wrong result. Initialize the best sum below every valid sum so negative inputs work.', 'O(n)','O(1)',3,['sum = 0; best = -infinity','for right in range(length):','  sum += values[right]','  if right >= k: sum -= values[right-k]','  if right >= k-1: best = max(best, sum)'],['Add the incoming value and subtract the outgoing one','Sort the whole window again','Recompute all previous windows'],0),
('binary-search','Keep the half that matters','Search','Binary search is a conversation with an ordered search space. Check the middle value. If it is smaller than the target, every position to its left can be eliminated too. Each comparison reduces the remaining interval by roughly half.','If the target exists, it remains inside the inclusive interval [low, high]. The loop terminates when the interval becomes empty.','Unsorted input invalidates the argument. Use low + (high-low)/2 and make progress after each comparison. Duplicates require a modified search for first or last occurrence.','O(log n)','O(1)',5,['low = 0; high = length - 1','while low <= high:','  mid = low + (high-low)//2','  if values[mid] == target: return mid','  keep only the possible half','return -1'],['The target remains within the current interval','The array gets sorted each step','Every value is checked'],0),
('merge-sort','Build order from smaller pieces','Sorting','Merge sort splits an interval into halves until each piece has at most one value. A single value is already sorted. The merge phase combines two sorted pieces by repeatedly taking their smaller next value.','Before a merge begins, both halves are sorted. The output prefix is sorted and contains the smallest values consumed so far.','Merging needs temporary storage. Choosing the left item on equal keys preserves stability. The animation shows completed merges rather than hiding the work in a library sort.','O(n log n)','O(n)',3,['split the interval in half','sort the left half','sort the right half','merge by taking the smaller next value','copy the merged interval back'],['Both halves are already sorted','Both halves have unique values','The pivot is the maximum'],0),
('quick-sort','Let a pivot organize the room','Sorting','Quicksort partitions values around a pivot. Values no larger than the pivot move to the left; larger ones remain on the right. Once the pivot is placed, recursively organize each side.','The partition boundary separates values already classified as small from those not yet examined. The pivot reaches a valid final position after partitioning.','A fixed last-element pivot can produce quadratic behavior on sorted data. This lesson deliberately exposes that behavior; the randomized-selection lesson shows random pivots.','Average O(n log n), worst O(n²)','Average O(log n) call stack',3,['choose the last value as pivot','scan the remaining interval','  move small values before the boundary','swap pivot into the boundary','recurse on each side'],['It can repeatedly split very unevenly','It never compares values','It needs an adjacency matrix'],0),
('linked-list','Turn the arrows around','Linked lists','A linked list stores relationships explicitly. To reverse it, keep previous, current, and next references. Save the next node before redirecting the current link, or the unprocessed suffix becomes unreachable.','Previous points to the reversed prefix. Current points to the first unprocessed node. Together they account for every original node.','The visual strip displays the reversed prefix as it grows. It is a conceptual linked structure, not an assertion that reversing an array has the same memory behavior.','O(n)','O(1)',3,['previous = null; current = head','while current exists:','  save next; current.next = previous','  previous = current; current = next','return previous'],['Save the next reference','Delete the head','Sort the nodes'],0),
('stack','The last thing in','Stacks & queues','A stack models last-in, first-out access. Push adds to the top; pop removes that same end. This simple discipline underlies matching brackets, undo histories, depth-first search, and the function call stack.','Only the top element is available for removal. Values below it keep their relative order.','Popping an empty stack needs explicit handling. This experiment pushes the input, then pops it so the reversal of order is visible.','O(1) per operation','O(n)',3,['create an empty stack','push each incoming value','inspect the top','pop the most recent value','stop when empty'],['The most recently pushed value','The smallest value','The oldest value'],0),
('queue','First come, first explored','Stacks & queues','A queue preserves arrival order. Enqueue at the back and dequeue at the front. It is the natural structure when earlier work must be processed before newer work, such as breadth-first search.','The oldest unprocessed item is always at the front. Enqueuing never changes the order of existing items.','Use a deque or linked queue in real implementations: removing index zero from an array shifts values. The visual strip shows logical queue behavior.','O(1) with a deque','O(n)',3,['create an empty queue','enqueue values at the back','inspect the front','dequeue the oldest value','stop when empty'],['The earliest enqueued value','The largest value','A random value'],0),
('recursion','One smaller question','Recursion','A recursive function delegates a smaller version of the same problem to itself. Factorial is a clear example: n! equals n times (n-1)!, with 0! equal to one. Suspended calls remain on the call stack.','Each call moves closer to a base case. During unwinding, every call receives the smaller result it needs.','A missing base case or a nondecreasing argument causes runaway recursion. The experiment caps n to keep the call stack readable.','O(n)','O(n) stack',5,['if n <= 1: return 1','ask factorial(n-1)','wait for the smaller answer','return n * smallerAnswer','unwind to the first call'],['It stops the recursion','It sorts the calls','It removes all memory use'],0),
('backtracking','Try a choice. Then undo it.','Recursion','Backtracking explores a decision tree while keeping only the current partial solution. In subset generation, choose a value, recursively explore what follows, then undo that choice before trying a different branch.','The current path contains precisely the choices made along the active branch. Undoing restores the state expected by the next branch.','Forgetting to undo leaks one branch into another. The number of subsets is exponential, so the experiment limits itself to the first five values.','O(n·2ⁿ) including output','O(n) excluding output',3,['record the current subset','for each available next value:','  choose the value','  explore the following choices','  undo the choice'],['Undo the last choice','Keep every choice forever','Restart the server'],0),
('bst','Give every value a place','Trees','A binary search tree organizes keys by order: smaller keys go left, larger keys go right. Search follows these comparisons rather than scanning every stored key. Shape depends on insertion order.','Every node separates smaller keys in its left subtree from larger keys in its right subtree. This experiment ignores duplicate insertions.','Sorted input can turn an ordinary BST into a chain. Height, rather than the mere presence of two child links, controls search cost.','O(h) per insert/search','O(n)',3,['begin at the root','compare with the current key','follow the smaller or larger branch','insert at an empty position','preserve the BST invariant'],['The height of the tree','The color of the diagram','Only the number of leaves'],0),
('avl','Keep the tree in balance','Trees','AVL trees add a local balance rule to the binary search invariant. After insertion, each node checks the difference between its left and right subtree heights. Rotations repair a difference greater than one while preserving key order.','For every node, the subtree heights differ by at most one. Rotations preserve the in-order sequence of keys.','Left-right and right-left cases need two rotations. Height updates must happen after pointer changes, starting with the lower node.','O(log n) per operation','O(n)',3,['insert using BST comparisons','update subtree heights','rotate an unbalanced subtree','preserve in-order key order','return the new subtree root'],['The in-order sequence','The exact root value','Every node depth'],0),
('tree-traversal','Visit a tree, level by level','Trees','Level-order traversal visits the root, then its children, then the next level. A queue records nodes awaiting a visit, maintaining the correct frontier between levels.','Every queued node has been discovered but not yet processed. Its children are added only when it is visited.','A stack changes the visitation order. The example first constructs a BST from the input so you can see how shape affects the traversal.','O(n)','O(w), maximum level width',3,['enqueue the root','while the queue is not empty:','  dequeue the next node','  visit it; enqueue its children','return the visit order'],['A queue','A minimum cut','A Bloom filter'],0),
('heap','Keep the priority within reach','Heaps','A max heap keeps each parent at least as large as its children. The maximum is immediately available at the root. Insertion appends a value, then moves it upward while it is larger than its parent.','The tree is complete, and the parent-child ordering holds everywhere except possibly along the new value’s path.','A heap is not a fully sorted array. Children have no required ordering relative to each other. Array positions encode the complete tree.','O(log n) insert, O(1) maximum','O(n)',3,['append the new value','compare it with its parent','swap while the parent is smaller','stop at a valid parent or root','read the maximum at index zero'],['The root is a maximum','The whole array is sorted','The left child is always smaller than the right'],0),
('bfs','Explore the nearest frontier','Graphs','Breadth-first search visits vertices in increasing unweighted distance from the starting vertex. A queue ensures that all vertices at one distance are explored before deeper ones.','Mark vertices when they enter the queue. A vertex is discovered once, even if several edges lead to it.','Edge weights are displayed but ignored by BFS. BFS does not generally find a minimum-weight path. The experiment derives a small directed graph from the numeric input.','O(V + E) with adjacency lists','O(V)',3,['enqueue and mark the source','while the queue is not empty:','  visit the next vertex','  enqueue unvisited neighbors','finish when no frontier remains'],['Minimum number of edges in an unweighted graph','Minimum weight in every graph','A sorted vertex list'],0),
('dfs','Follow a branch further','Graphs','Depth-first search uses the newest pending branch first. It explores reachability and is a building block for cycle detection, topological ordering, and connected-component analysis.','A vertex is marked on discovery to prevent repeated processing. A stack holds the pending frontier.','Changing adjacency order changes the valid traversal order. DFS is not a shortest-path algorithm. This experiment uses an explicit stack.','O(V + E) with adjacency lists','O(V)',3,['push and mark the source','while the stack is not empty:','  visit the newest pending vertex','  push undiscovered neighbors','continue until the frontier is empty'],['It explores a branch before older pending branches','It always finds the lightest path','It sorts all edges'],0),
('dijkstra','Always settle the nearest','Graphs','Dijkstra’s algorithm repeatedly chooses the unsettled vertex with the smallest known distance. Relaxing an outgoing edge checks whether reaching a neighbor through this vertex improves its current estimate.','With nonnegative edge weights, a settled distance cannot later improve. This is the reason greedy finalization is valid.','Negative weights break that reasoning. This readable implementation scans for the minimum, giving O(V²) time; a heap-based implementation changes the bound.','O(V² + E) in this implementation','O(V²) matrix representation',3,['distance[source] = 0','choose the nearest unsettled vertex','relax its outgoing edges','mark its distance final','repeat for reachable vertices'],['Nonnegative edge weights','Equal vertex names','A complete graph'],0),
('max-flow','Find room for one more path','Graphs','A flow network assigns capacities to directed edges. Edmonds–Karp finds a source-to-sink path in the residual network, sends its bottleneck capacity, and repeats. Reverse residual edges let later paths undo and reroute earlier choices.','Flow respects capacity and conservation. Residual capacity records what can still be added or reversed.','Ignoring reverse edges can leave a nonmaximum flow. When no augmenting path remains, residual reachability identifies a minimum cut.','O(V·E²)','O(V²) here',3,['find a residual path with BFS','compute its bottleneck capacity','reduce forward residual capacities','increase reverse capacities; add flow','stop when no path remains'],['They allow earlier flow to be rerouted','They double every capacity','They make the graph undirected'],0),
('memoization','Remember the answer you earned','Dynamic programming','Naive recursive Fibonacci repeatedly solves the same subproblems. Memoization caches an answer the first time it is computed, then turns repeated calls into lookups.','A cached state is correct once all dependencies have been computed correctly. The recursive structure remains visible.','Caching the wrong state key returns plausible but incorrect results. Zero may be a valid value, so a general cache should distinguish absent from computed zero.','O(n)','O(n)',8,['return directly for a base case','reuse a cached value if present','solve the two smaller states','store their sum','return the stored answer'],['Repeated subproblem evaluation','All recursion automatically','The need for a correct recurrence'],0),
('tabulation','Build the answer from the bottom','Dynamic programming','Tabulation starts with base states and fills a table in dependency order. For Fibonacci, each value uses the two previous entries. The table makes the reuse of work explicit.','Before filling state i, every state it depends on is already available. The iteration order follows the dependency graph.','A correct recurrence with the wrong loop order can still be wrong. Some tables can be compressed after the dependency pattern is understood.','O(n)','O(n), compressible to O(1)',8,['initialize base states','for each later state:','  combine the previous two values','store the new result','read the final table entry'],['Dependency order','Alphabetical order','Random order in every problem'],0),
('trie','Let words share their beginnings','Text search','A trie stores a character per edge so that words with a common prefix share a path. A prefix query follows only the characters typed so far, then enumerates completions below that point.','Each root-to-node path spells a prefix. A terminal marker distinguishes a complete word from a prefix that is not itself a word.','Prefix lookup cost and result enumeration cost are different. Returning many completions costs at least as much as writing those results.','O(L) lookup plus output','O(total stored characters)',3,['start at the root','for each character:','  follow or create the matching edge','mark the final node as a word','enumerate beneath a query prefix'],['A word-ending marker','A separate sorted array','A negative capacity'],0),
('set-cover','Cover more with each choice','Greedy algorithms','Practice questions often teach several topics. To cover a selected set of topics with a compact plan, repeatedly choose the question adding the most still-uncovered topics. That is a unit-cost greedy set-cover approximation.','The covered set grows after every useful choice. The algorithm stops with complete coverage or identifies topics no remaining question can cover.','The result is not guaranteed to use the fewest questions. Under unit costs and feasible coverage, the standard greedy bound is harmonic in the number of topics.','O(P·T·min(P,T)) for straightforward sets','O(P·T)',3,['start with all requested topics uncovered','evaluate each candidate’s new coverage','choose the greatest gain','remove covered topics; repeat','report any uncovered topics'],['It is an approximation, not always optimal','It always finds the exact minimum','It ignores the selected topics'],0),
('quickselect','Find the middle without sorting it all','Randomization','Randomized Quickselect chooses a pivot and partitions around it. Only the side containing the requested order statistic needs further work. The rest of the values can stay unordered.','After partitioning, the pivot’s position tells us which interval can contain the requested rank. Random pivots make repeatedly bad splits unlikely.','Expected linear time is not a worst-case guarantee. The production selector uses three-way partitioning to handle duplicates efficiently; this small lesson shows pivot movement explicitly.','Expected O(n), worst O(n²)','O(n) defensive copy',3,['choose a random pivot','partition the current interval','compare pivot position with k','keep only the relevant side','return when the pivot reaches k'],['Only the side containing the requested rank','Both sides must be fully sorted','Only the smaller values, always'],0),
('bloom-filter','A useful kind of uncertainty','Probabilistic structures','A Bloom filter sets several bit positions for each inserted identifier. A missing bit proves absence. If every bit is set, the identifier might be present because other identifiers may have set the same positions.','With insert-only operation, a previously inserted identifier has all its bits set. Collisions can create false positives, not false negatives.','A positive result cannot justify discarding an item when exact membership matters. Confirm it against authoritative storage. Ordinary bit clearing is not a safe deletion operation.','O(k) per operation','O(m) bits',3,['start with all bits zero','compute the item’s hash positions','set each selected bit','a missing bit means absent','all set means possibly present'],['Possibly present; confirm exactly when needed','Definitely present','Definitely absent'],0)
]
